 terminal chow (W1-2-3) or a pung if I draw another, but W2 (3 left) and W3 (4 left) are possible. Discarding W1 reduces terminal count for All Simples.  \n3. **Play B7** \u2013 B7 is a terminal (number 7), only 2 left (I have 2), but B8 and B9 are still available (B8:3 left, B9:3 left), so a B7-8-9 chow is possible, but across player already has B7-8-9 chow, so less B7 available.  \n4. **Play T4, T6, etc.** \u2013 T4 and T6 are simples (4,6), keeping them could help form chows in T suit.  \n\nGiven scoring potential:  \n- If I discard honors and terminals, I can aim for \u201cAll Simples\u201d + \u201cNo Honors\u201d (total 3 points) plus other chows/pungs patterns.  \n- The exposed Chow [B1, B2, B3] is a simple chow, so I can build a hand with \u201cAll Chows\u201d (2 points) + \u201cAll Simples\u201d (2 points) + \u201cNo Honors\u201d (1 point) = 5 points, still need 3 more.  \n- Additional patterns like \u201cPure Double Chow\u201d (if I get two identical chows) or \u201cMixed Double Chow\u201d or \u201cShort Straight\u201d could add 1\u20132 points each.  \n\nBest immediate discard: **F4** \u2013 because it\u2019s an honor not useful for my seat/prevalent wind, only 1 left, so almost no chance for pung; discarding it helps reduce honors for potential \u201cNo Honors\u201d pattern.  \n\nAfter that, I can work on forming chows in W and T suits (e.g., W6-7-8, T4-5-6, etc.) using remaining tiles.  \n\n**Final choice:** Play F4.\n\n**Reason:** Discarding F4 removes an honor tile that is unlikely to contribute to a meld given only 1 left in the wall, and it helps pursue patterns like No Honors and All Simples, which are easier to achieve with my current simple-suited tiles.\n\n**Answer:** Play F4"}
